 is have that building collapse  and kill 10 20 30 50 firefighters  or wound them or first responders  so uh but mike they're you know they're  realistic i  it just brought back so many  so many memories it's bad enough  it's bad enough to lose somebody  but the hard part the really hard part  is to not know whether they're surviving  or not  just not having the idea  when the accident took my wife my family  the hardest part was  were my boys going to get out because  they're going to make it  um did not know it not knowing when  you're flying home from washington to  get the news you know i just don't know  so it's uh but i was amazed  as you know unfortunately  i've done a lot of these circumstances  where i've met with families who have  had great laws  and what amazed me about this group of  people  was their resilience their absolute  commitment their willingness to do  whatever it took  to find uh to find an  answer i i walked away  impressed uh by their strength  and uh um nancy bloomberg do you have a  question  i was told oh thank you um yeah what did  you learn if anything about the collapse  of the building is there anything more  you learn from investigators or the fema  administration no it's underway  i don't uh the director of fema is with  me here we don't have any  firm proof of what's happened there's  all kinds of uh  rational speculation about whether or  not  the rebars are rusted whether or not the  cement  uh whether it's limestone or not whether  or not  but a lot of the a lot of families who  survived  talked about how upset they were that  in the last years that they've been here  how  there was one condominium complex built  across  the street and a road was purchased and  while they were living there they would  hear the drilling  and they'd feel their building moving  and shaking  uh there are all kinds of discussions  about whether or not  um they uh they thought that water level  rising what impact it had  and interesting to me i didn